Well, we haven't had a YAFAP for a while. This one just confirmed to
me the old saying about the game getting easier later on (this is the
first time I got past the Castle, and it lead to my first ascension).
I also got a couple of conducts by chance (no wishing for artifacts,
no changing my own form), and also kept to a few non-conducts; for
instance, I didn't kill or harm shopkeepers all game, not even with my
pets, and I didn't get sleep resistance at any point (I forgot about
it...). Also unusual was that I ascended without telepathy (due to
murdering the priest of Moloch in the VotD, I'd forgotten that it was
a bad enemy to kill...), and without any divine protection (I never
bought any).

The start of the game was typical; I did Sokoban first and got the
"oR. I'd found a bag of holding in a shop on Dlvl 4, but couldn't
afford it at the time; I can't remember offhand whether I got my pet
to steal it or came back and bought it later. There were two magic
lamps and exactly 7 candles in Izchak's, which was useful (I left the
candles around until later, but bought the lamps). I'd also tamed a
warhorse near the Sokoban entrance, and found a saddle in a shop, so I
rode around on the warhorse for a while. (Embarassingly, I forgot that
#ride untames; so I ended up with a peaceful saddled warhorse called
Ned in the Mines, and then spent ages finding a lichen corpse to
retame it with so I could get back in the saddle; normally, I don't
kill peacefuls, apart from the Titan on the Medusa level (because it
tends to go hostile later on, it's just self-preservation) and tengu
(although I didn't get intrinsics from either the tengu I killed). Ned
eventually died, later on.

I eventually (at Xlvl 10 and Dlvl 15 IIRC) ended up using one of the
magic lamps to wish for a blessed unihorn, as I'd been poisoned down
to 10 Str and still didn't have poison resistance, nor could I find an
enemy unicorn to get a horn from. The unihorn healed me back up to
full strength and allows me to safely gain poison resistance once I
found the first of 5 beehives in the game (a personal record; that
18/57 Str on my stats at the bottom is all intrinsic, and mostly due
to royal jelly). Normally I wouldn't spend a wish on something as
common as a unihorn, but I decided that I really badly needed poison
resistance. (The second magic lamp went on GDSM some time later, when
I needed to unburden myself.)

I did the Quest before descending below Medusa level (I normally it
that way round), gaining most of the levels needed through combat
(with a b!oGL to reach level 14, and I soon after gained 15 due to
ending up near the high end of the range). Surtur was unexpectedly
easy; he wasn't even at the stairs when I killed him. I also found a
shield of reflection just next to the Quest portal; I didn't use it
until much later in the game, but I kept it around with me. I killed
Medusa by digging down and applying a mirror at her (I had intrinsic
invisibility by that point). (Perseus provided only cursed boots of
levitation, which I dropped in favour of some unBCUed boots of
levitation (which were also, as usual, cursed, but I thought it was
worth the tiny chance of not having to spend an uncursing item), and a
sack, which I ascended with.) All through the game, I used the Orb of
Fate's level-teleport to get back above Medusa (apart from the
ascension run, when I jumped across the Medusa level rather than
levitating; I used jumping boots pretty much throughout the game).
Throughout the whole game, I had a stash in Sokoban where I kept most
of my items, and didn't create any other stashes; every time I
#invoked my way out of trouble (for instance, when I unexpectedly
found myself next to a purple L), I would go back to the stash and do
something that would avoid the trouble happening again (in that case,
wishing for blessed genocide and genociding L; the wish was to
identify the scrolls, more than anything else, and I used the second
scroll from the wish on ; some time later). On other stash-trips I
enchanted my armor, or my weapons; for ages I wore burnt +0 leather
gloves due to not having any better gloves and wanting a safety net
against walking on trice corpses when blind, but I found a pair of
gauntlets of Dex in the Castle, and enchanted and fooproofed them. (Of
the armour I ascended with, the cloak and mail were wished for, the
helmet and gloves came from the Castle storeroom, and I'd found the
boots and shields earlier. No T-shirt, but the resulting AC was good
enough to make me pretty survivable.

I'd found plenty of instruments, and used a magic flute to open the
drawbridge Mastermind-style (and then drawbridge-danced to clear out
much of the Castle); unfortunately a soldier blew up the drawbridge
with a wand of striking, so from then on I just cleared out the Castle
in melee. The wand gave me many useful items; I spent one wish on a
blessed figurine of a ki-rin just for fun, and did most of Gehennom
flying, until Orcus killed it with his wand of death. Other wishes
went on scrolls of charging (as always), and markers (which I mostly
used to write gold detection (for the Planes), enchant armour, and
enchant weapon; I also wrote a couple of scrolls of identify, and on
my last trip to the stash during the ascension run I wrote a blessed
scroll of remove curse as an anti-malignant aura tactic).

I did Gehennom the good old-fashioned tedious way; I #twoweaponed
Excalibur (which I'd dipped for after Sokoban) and silver saber until
I found an amulet of life saving, then switched to Excalibur and a
shield of reflection. (I never needed the life saving all game, but I
used it as a safety net against typos; there was a second amulet of
life saving in Vlad's, as always, but that stayed in my bag of holding
all game.) Most of my time in Gehennom was spent mapping by
exploration, pickaxeing (or later on using wands of digging because I
had about 20 of them, and I dropped them once empty) a short path to
save time on the ascension run, and occasionally #invoking my way out
of trouble.

I killed all the named demons by hand; Jubilex was killed in a few
rounds from inside, and then I unihorned myself back to health, but
most of the others were more difficult, involving a chase through
several levels to set up a situation where it was me standing on the
upstair not the demon. Probably my biggest mistake was accidentally
blanking all my scrolls and potions going back through the Jubilex
level some time later, due to typoing myself into the water several
times, but nothing all that valuable was lost, and used some of my
scrolls of enchant armor to rustproof and fix the rust damage.
(Everything I was wearing but the shield of reflection - which doesn't
get damaged, was fooproofed at the end of the game.) I used a
forgotten spell (the only one I bothered learning, as I stayed on 3 Pw
all game due to being a dwarven valk) to confuse myself when
necessary.

As for the Wizard of Yendor, I killed him (both the original time and
every time afterwards) with a wand of death; I recharged my randomly
generated wand of death during the ascension run when it ran out of
charges, to continue killing him. The Sanctum was relatively easy; I
just stood at a chokepoint and killed everything one at a time as it
came through, then walked over to Moloch's high altar, used a wand of
secret door detection to find the entrance, and meleed the High Priest
of Moloch to death. (The fire traps were no problem; I had fire
resistance and there was nothing in my main inventory they affected.)
I dropped the invocation artifacts soon afterwards to save weight, and
then all my money.

Once I had the amulet, I went up by walking, pretty much, and jumping
over obstacles. As a lawful, the mysterious force was really annoying,
but I managed to reach the Castle eventually and from there it was
just a case of running up (and jumping through the Medusa level).

The Planes were not too difficult; confused gold detection found the
portals, and I mostly just avoided the enemies, using a tooled horn to
scare them away. (Tooled horns are particularly useful in Air.) I also
used a ring of conflict that I'd found in Gehennom throughout all 5
Planes (possibly not Earth, I'm not sure, but the others, anyway). In
Astral, I killed Pestilence by hand twice, and Famine died twice from
the conflict; I did Pestilence first, who was guarding the wrong
altar, but Famine's altar was the correct one. (I used a quaffed
potion of speed and #jump to move through the plane as quickly as
possible, and only killed things when they were in the way; I also had
some potions of full healing out for the rare occasions when I was
surrounded and thus taking damage faster than I was regenerating it,
but normally due to half physical damage, half magical damage, -34 AC,
and a really high Constitution, the enemies couldn't damage me as fast
as my natural healing.)



